//
// Normative rule H_cause_virtual_instruction (src/priv/hypervisor.adoc):
//   When V=1, a virtual-instruction exception (code 22) is normally raised instead of an illegal-
//     instruction exception if the attempted instruction is HS-qualified but is prevented from
//     executing when V=1 either due to insufficient privilege or because the instruction is
//     expressly disabled by a supervisor or hypervisor CSR such as scounteren or hcounteren. An
//     instruction is HS-qualified if it would be valid to execute in HS-mode (for some values of
//     the instruction's register operands), assuming fields TSR and TVM of CSR mstatus are both
//     zero.
//
// Context:
//   The positive cases are enumerated in the H_virtinst_* rules. This test checks the boundary:
//     instructions that are not valid in HS-mode raise illegal-instruction from VS and VU, and HS-
//     qualified ones raise virtual-instruction.
//
// Clarifications:
//   HS-qualified (hypervisor.adoc, Virtual Instruction): an instruction is HS-qualified if it would
//     be valid in HS-mode for some register operands, assuming mstatus.TVM=0 and TSR=0. A CSR write
//     that HS-mode may not do (a read-only CSR) is not HS-qualified, so it raises illegal-
//     instruction from VS and VU too.
//
// Test plan:
//   1. M-level CSRs (mstatus, misa, medeleg, mideleg, mie, mtvec, mcounteren, mscratch, mepc,
//     mcause, mtval, mip, mtinst, mtval2, mhartid): a VS-mode and a VU-mode read raise illegal-
//     instruction.
//   2. MRET from VS and VU raises illegal-instruction; so do MNRET and DRET (not valid outside
//     M-mode or Debug Mode).
//   3. Writes to read-only counters (cycle, time, instret with Zicntr) from VS-mode, with
//     mcounteren and hcounteren all set, raise illegal-instruction (HS-mode may not write them
//     either).
//   4. Contrast, HS-qualified: SRET from VU-mode and a VU-mode read of sscratch raise virtual-
//     instruction.
//
// Approximate dynamic instruction count: 10631
//

